-- PINNED REPRO — `sky check` clean, `go build` clean, PANICS at runtime.
--
-- Every reference below is FULLY QUALIFIED. Nothing is ambiguously imported, so
-- no use-site ambiguity rule — `[E1012]` in any namespace — can reach this.
--
-- `Conflate.Alpha.Shape` and `Conflate.Beta.Shape` are two different unions with
-- disjoint constructors. The type checker cannot tell them apart: every
-- non-alias type collapses to its bare final segment
-- (`ty::sig::rewrite_alias_refs` module-qualifies `DefKind::TypeAlias` ONLY),
-- and `unify.rs` compares `Ty::App` names as plain strings. So `App("Shape")`
-- from Alpha unifies with `App("Shape")` from Beta and the call below is
-- "well typed".
--
-- Lowering, meanwhile, DOES distinguish them — it emits `Conflate_Alpha_Shape`
-- and `Conflate_Beta_Shape` as separate Go interfaces — and bridges the gap with
-- `rt.Coerce[Conflate_Beta_Shape](Conflate_Alpha_Shape_Circle(3))`. That
-- assertion succeeds, because both Go types are interfaces with the same method
-- set, and hands `betaTag` a value none of its arms match:
--
--     panic: sky.Unreachable(case): sky: codegen reached an arm the
--            exhaustiveness checker said was impossible
--
-- expect: REJECT (or a type error). Actual today: accept, then CompilerBug panic.
--
-- This violates "no runtime panic from well-typed Sky code". It is NOT closed by
-- the type-namespace extension of `[E1012]`, and it is filed here so the gap is
-- visible rather than remembered — see `docs/rust-rewrite/05-name-resolution.md`
-- §"What this does NOT close".
--
-- CHECKED IN, hand-written. Not generator output.
module Main exposing (main)

import Sky.Core.Prelude exposing (..)
import Std.Log exposing (println)
import Conflate.Alpha as A
import Conflate.Beta as B


main =
    println (B.betaTag (A.Circle 3))
